TEE(1)                         P32W Unix                          TEE(1)

NAME
       tee - copy the standard input to files

SYNOPSIS
       tee [-ai] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       tee copies its standard input to its standard output and
       also to each named file, which is created or truncated
       (mode 666, less the umask). It is used to keep a copy of
       data passing through a pipeline. Data is passed on as it
       arrives, up to 512 bytes at a time.

       A file that cannot be created, or that a write fails on
       (the standard output too), is reported and left out from
       then on; the copying goes on to the others. As many files
       can be written as the process may have open (20 descriptors,
       three of them the standard ones).

OPTIONS
       -a     append to the files instead of truncating them
       -i     ignore interrupts (SIGINT), so that tee goes on
              when the command it is reading from is interrupted

EXAMPLES
       who | tee who.out             see the list, and keep it
       ls -l | tee list | wc -l
       make 2>&1 | tee -a make.log

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 if a file could not be opened or written, for a read
       error, or for a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       cat(1), sh(1)

BUGS
       If the standard output is a pipe whose reader has gone, tee
       is stopped by SIGPIPE, like any program, and the files get
       no more of the data.
